d (or no children at all).\n  - If conditions satisfied:\n    - Remove node: add to removed set.\n    - Get parent id (if not null). Parent is not removed (since we only remove leaves, parent can't be removed yet). Enqueue parent if it is not already enqueued and not removed. (But parent might already be in queue; we can check enqueued set to avoid duplicate). However, note that after removal, parent may become a leaf itself, and we enqueue it for reconsideration. So we add parent to enqueued set and push onto heap.\n- After loop, retained_node_ids = list of node ids not in removed. Order? The output schema just says array of strings, order not specified. But likely we should return them in some order? The description does not specify order. We can sort them maybe by ID or depth? But \"retained_node_ids\" is just a set, but output as array. Usually they might expect the IDs that were never removed. We can return them in any order, but to be consistent, we can return sorted by ID? Or keep in order of appearance? Let's look at examples? There are no expected outputs provided. However, we can infer from the task: \"Return retained IDs and the complete visit order.\" It's reasonable to return retained IDs in arbitrary order, but maybe they expect in the order they appear in the original nodes list? But better to sort them by ID or depth? I'll return them in the order they were original? Actually the original nodes list order might be meaningful. But the output schema doesn't specify any sort. I'll return them in the order they appear in the nodes list (since that's the input order), but that might be arbitrary. To be safe, I'll return them sorted by ID (string). Many such problems expect sorted order. Let's check the contract: \"retained_node_ids\" is an array of strings. No required order. I'll sort them lexicographically for determinism.\n\n- visit_order: exactly the order nodes were popped from the heap.\n\nEdge cases:\n- Root node has parent null. Root is not removable (removable false). So root will never be removed. But might be enqueued if child removed? Yes, we enqueue parent after child removal. Root will be enqueued. But root is not removable so it won't be removed. That's fine.\n\n- Nodes that are not removable are never removed even if leaf and utility low.\n\n- Utility threshold: utility <= threshold (float compare, but direct comparison is fine).\n\n- The queue should only contain nodes that are not removed. When we pop a node that has been removed (because we might have enqueued parent later but later parent might be removed? Actually we only enqueue parent if parent is not removed and not already enqueued. So parent should not be removed at that time. But after enqueuing, the parent might later be removed? But we only remove leaves, so if parent is removed, its children must have been removed first. So if parent is enqueued later, it cannot be removed until we process it. So no conflict.\n\n- However, there could be duplicate pushes if the same node is enqueued multiple times from different children removals. We prevent that with enqueued set. But careful: After we process a node and do not remove it, we should not later enqueue it again? The algorithm: after removal, we enqueue parent. The parent is already in queue maybe earlier? But if it was already processed earlier (popped and not removed), then we are enqueuing it again. That's allowed: we need to reconsider it because its children changed (some removed). So we should allow re-enqueuing after it's been popped. The enqueued set should be cleared when we pop? Actually we need to allow a node to be re-added to queue after it has been popped (if it wasn't removed). So we should not use enqueued set that persists across pops. Instead, we should only avoid adding the same node multiple times while it is still in the queue. Since we are using a heap, we cannot easily check if it's already in the heap. A common approach: maintain a 'in_queue' set that we clear when 