"n4\", \"n5\", \"n6\", \"root\". Lexicographically, \"n1\" < \"n10\"? But we don't have such. It will be fine.\n\nNow, need to implement solve(case) that returns JSON. Use Python standard library only: heapq, json? Actually returning dict, which will be serialized by evaluator to JSON. We don't need to json.dumps inside solve. The function must return a JSON-serializable object (dict). So we can return a dict.\n\nAlso, we must not mutate case. So we can deep copy? But we are only reading from case, not mutating. So fine.\n\nEdge: The case payload may have _case_nonce field; ignore it.\n\nNow, write the code.\n\nOne more thing: The output schema says \"retained_node_ids\" and \"visit_order\" both arrays of strings. We must ensure node ids are strings (they are).\n\nNow produce the final output: a JSON object with key \"implementation\" whose value is the Python source code as a JSON string. Need to escape newlines, backslashes, quotes. We'll write the code in a multi-line string and then json.dumps it. We'll enclose in the envelope.\n\nLet's write the Python code:\n\n```python\nimport heapq\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    threshold = case['utility_threshold']\n    node_map = {}\n    children = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        nid = n['id']\n        node_map[nid] = n\n        children.setdefault(nid, [])\n    for n in nodes:\n        pid = n['parent']\n        if pid is not None:\n            children.setdefault(pid, []).append(n['id'])\n    \n    # initial queue sort by depth desc, then id asc\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda x: (-x['depth'], x['id']))\n    heap = []\n    in_queue = set()\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\n        heapq.heappush(heap, (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n        in_queue.add(n['id'])\n    \n    removed = set()\n    visit_order = []\n    \n    while heap:\n        neg_depth, nid = heapq.heappop(heap)\n        in_queue.discard(nid)\n        visit_order.append(nid)\n        if nid in removed:\n            continue\n        node = node_map[nid]\n        # check removable leaf condition\n        if node['removable'] and node['utility'] <= threshold:\n            # check leaf: all children removed?\n            is_leaf = True\n            for child_id in children.get(nid, []):\n                if child_id not in removed:\n                    is_leaf = False\n                    break\n            if is_leaf:\n                removed.add(nid)\n                parent_id = node.get('parent')\n                if parent_id is not None and parent_id not in removed:\n                    if parent_id not in in_queue:\n                        parent_node = node_map[parent_id]\n                        heapq.heappush(heap, (-parent_node['depth'], parent_id))\n                        in_queue.add(parent_id)\n    \n    retained = [n['id'] for n in nodes if n['id'] not in removed]\n    return {\n        'retained_node_ids': retained,\n        'visit_order': visit_order\n    }\n```\n\nBut note: The initial queue includes all nodes. However, the root \"root\" is depth 0. It might be at the end of heap due to largest depth? Actually depth descending, so depth 0 is smallest, so root will be at bottom of heap. That's fine; we process deepest nodes first.\n\nPotential issue: Node IDs as strings, like \"n6\" etc. The sorting by id ascending string will treat \"n10\" < \"n2\"? Not relevant.\n\nWe also need to be careful about children list: we built children mapping by iterating nodes. That covers all nodes. For nodes with no children, children.get(nid, []) will return empty list, so leaf check passes.\n\nComplexity: O(N log N + M log M) where M is number of pushes.\n\nNow produce the JSON envelope.\n\nWe need to escape the code string. I'll produce the JSON manually or use a helper. Since this is a text response, I'll manually write the JSON with escaped newlines (\\\\n) and quotes.\n\nI'll write:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"import heapq\\n\\ndef solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    threshold = case